        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(synology-chat): evict stale chat user cache entries",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes an issue where the `chatUserCache` Map in Synology Chat could leak memory when callers rotated through many webhook URLs on the same NAS. The cache checked TTL only on reads; stale entries from old URLs on the same origin were never removed from the Map, so a hostile or misconfigured caller cycling through webhook URLs on the same NAS could grow the Map without bound for the lifetime of the process.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThis is a root-cause fix at the cache insertion boundary: before inserting a new entry, `fetchChatUsers` now evicts stale entries scoped to the same origin as the current request. Different origins (e.g. `https://nas1.example.com` and `https://nas2.example.com`) are isolated — a successful refresh for endpoint B cannot delete endpoint A's last-known-good fallback. The stale-on-error contract in the failure branches (parse failure, API error, HTTP error, timeout) still returns `cached?.users ?? []` for its own endpoint regardless of what happened on another origin.\n\nThe change reuses the existing `CACHE_TTL_MS` constant and `cachedAt` timestamp already present on every cache entry. No config, schema, storage, or public API surface changes; the read path (freshness check before returning cached data) is unchanged.\n\n## User Impact\n\nSynology Chat channels with multiple NAS endpoints no longer risk cross-endpoint fallback loss from unrelated successful refreshes. Same-NAS multi-webhook deployments still benefit from stale-entry eviction. Single-URL deployments are unchanged — the eviction pass only fires on successful cache writes, and with one entry there is no other entry to evict.\n\n## Evidence\n\n### TDD: cross-origin preservation (P1 regression fix)\n\nBefore fix — endpoint B's refresh evicts endpoint A's stale fallback:\n\n```\nFAIL |extension-messaging| extensions/synology-chat/src/client.test.ts > fetchChatUsers > preserves other-origin stale cache entries on cross-origin refresh\nAssertionError: expected false to be true // Object.is equality\n\n- Expected\n+ Received\n\n- true\n+ false\n\n ❯ extensions/synology-chat/src/client.test.ts:507:39\n    506|   // Verify A's stale entry is preserved (fallback for failed refresh)\n    507|   expect(chatUserCache.has(listUrlA)).toBe(true);\n       |                                       ^\n    508|\n\n Test Files  1 failed (1)\n      Tests  1 failed | 23 passed (24)\n```\n\nAfter fix — endpoint B's refresh preserves endpoint A's stale fallback:\n\n```\n ✓ |extension-messaging| extensions/synology-chat/src/client.test.ts > fetchChatUsers > preserves other-origin stale cache entries on cross-origin refresh 1ms\n\n Test Files  1 passed (1)\n      Tests  24 passed (24)\n```\n\n### Same-origin eviction still works\n\n```\n ✓ |extension-messaging| extensions/synology-chat/src/client.test.ts > fetchChatUsers > evicts stale same-origin cache entries when inserting a fresh one 1ms\n```\n\n### Scoped checks\n\n```\noxlint: pass\ngit diff --check: pass\n```\n",
